1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:12,187
I am hot. I am hot. Forty one. Forty one. Am I going to crash? Yes. Yes. I am hot.

2
00:00:12,187 --> 00:00:13,965
I will re-enter.

3
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:23,137
In 1961, two brothers in a listening station outside Turin pulled a voice like that out of the static.

4
00:00:23,137 --> 00:00:26,097
A woman, they said. In orbit. Burning.

5
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:35,632
What you just heard is a recreation of what they logged, because the Soviet Union said that woman never existed.

6
00:00:35,632 --> 00:00:38,152
No launch. No cosmonaut. No voice.

7
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:40,739
Here is the problem.

8
00:00:40,739 --> 00:00:45,416
The Soviet Union said the same thing about men who provably did exist.

9
00:00:45,416 --> 00:00:50,454
A man who burned alive in a training chamber, hidden for twenty five years.

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:58,679
A man cut out of official photographs so cleanly that for decades, historians did not know he had ever been there.

11
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:05,618
This is the story of six erasures. Six times the Soviet space program made men disappear.

12
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:08,891
Number four is the one they could not hide.

13
00:01:10,033 --> 00:01:19,124
By the end, you will know what is proven, what was buried, and what is still sitting on nine recordings from Italy, argued over for sixty years.

14
00:01:20,266 --> 00:01:23,427
Erasure number one. The day the steppe burned.

15
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:32,448
October 1960. The Cold War is a scoreboard, and the Soviet Union is winning it. Sputnik.

16
00:01:32,448 --> 00:01:38,359
Laika. The far side of the Moon. Every first belongs to Moscow.

17
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,805
Behind the propaganda, the pressure is crushing.

18
00:01:42,805 --> 00:01:50,359
Premier Khrushchev wants a new intercontinental missile, the R-16, ready for the anniversary of the revolution.

19
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:52,247
The deadline is impossible.

20
00:01:53,366 --> 00:02:03,162
On October 24th, the R-16 stands fueled on the pad at Baikonur, loaded with hypergolic propellants so vicious the engineers call them devil's venom.

21
00:02:04,299 --> 00:02:11,405
The missile is leaking. Safe procedure says drain it and start over, a delay of weeks.

22
00:02:11,405 --> 00:02:15,846
Marshal Mitrofan Nedelin, commander of the strategic rocket forces, refuses.

23
00:02:16,966 --> 00:02:21,484
He orders the fault fixed on the pad. On a live, fueled missile.

24
00:02:21,484 --> 00:02:28,434
And to prove how safe he thinks it is, he has a chair carried out and sits down beside it.

25
00:02:29,566 --> 00:02:35,175
Around him, dozens of engineers and soldiers are working on the rocket like mechanics under a car.

26
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:38,474
Nobody wants to look slower than the Marshal is patient.

27
00:02:39,599 --> 00:02:47,209
At a quarter to seven in the evening, a botched reset of the missile's flight programmer fires the second stage engine.

28
00:02:47,209 --> 00:02:50,832
Straight down. Into the fully fueled first stage below it.

29
00:02:51,966 --> 00:02:54,797
The fireball swallows the pad in seconds.

30
00:02:54,797 --> 00:02:58,437
Men in fuel soaked overalls simply cease to exist.

31
00:02:58,437 --> 00:03:03,695
Others run burning across the concrete until the wire perimeter fence stops them.

32
00:03:04,833 --> 00:03:09,147
Automatic cameras, set up to film a triumph, film all of it.

33
00:03:09,147 --> 00:03:12,382
The footage is seized and classified the same night.

34
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:19,377
Marshal Nedelin, in his chair, is identified afterward by a medal that survived the fire.

35
00:03:19,377 --> 00:03:23,296
The official death toll, admitted decades later, is seventy eight.

36
00:03:23,296 --> 00:03:26,430
Other counts go past one hundred and twenty.

37
00:03:27,566 --> 00:03:32,185
And then comes the part that defines everything you will see in this video.

38
00:03:32,185 --> 00:03:36,474
The Soviet Union does not announce a disaster. It announces a plane crash.

39
00:03:37,599 --> 00:03:43,224
Soviet newspapers report that Marshal Nedelin has died in an aviation accident. One man.

40
00:03:43,224 --> 00:03:49,250
One obituary. The other victims are buried quietly, some in a mass grave at Baikonur.

41
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:55,056
Families are told their sons died in accidents that never happened.

42
00:03:55,056 --> 00:04:00,560
The largest launch pad disaster in history officially does not exist until 1989.

43
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:02,677
Twenty nine years of silence.

44
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:08,659
Remember that reflex. When something dies at Baikonur, the truth dies with it.

45
00:04:08,659 --> 00:04:14,641
Because five months later, the machine that erased a hundred men gets a much smaller job.

46
00:04:15,766 --> 00:04:19,554
Erasure number two. The fire nobody was told about.

47
00:04:20,699 --> 00:04:21,410
March 1961.

48
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:27,097
Twenty young pilots are training in secret outside Moscow for something no human has ever done.

49
00:04:27,097 --> 00:04:32,428
One of them will be first in space. The world knows none of their names.

50
00:04:33,566 --> 00:04:35,782
The youngest is Valentin Bondarenko.

51
00:04:35,782 --> 00:04:42,872
Twenty four years old, a fighter pilot, by every account the kindest man in the group.

52
00:04:42,872 --> 00:04:45,530
They call him Zvonochek. Little bell.

53
00:04:46,666 --> 00:04:56,227
On March 23rd he is finishing day ten of a fifteen day isolation test, sealed in a low pressure chamber where much of the air has been replaced with oxygen.

54
00:04:57,366 --> 00:05:05,962
At the end of a medical session, he peels the sensors off his skin and wipes the adhesive away with a piece of cotton soaked in alcohol.

55
00:05:05,962 --> 00:05:07,554
Then he tosses it aside.

56
00:05:08,699 --> 00:05:11,834
It lands on the coil of an electric hotplate.

57
00:05:11,834 --> 00:05:16,362
In oxygen that rich, there is no such thing as a small flame.

58
00:05:16,362 --> 00:05:18,103
The chamber ignites around him.

59
00:05:19,233 --> 00:05:23,395
The chamber is pressurized. The door cannot simply be pulled open.

60
00:05:23,395 --> 00:05:30,962
It takes agonizing minutes to equalize and unseal it while he burns inside, in a wool training suit, on camera.

61
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:37,581
When they finally reach him, Bondarenko is still conscious. Still on his feet.

62
00:05:37,581 --> 00:05:43,907
He keeps whispering the same thing to the doctors. It's my fault. It's my fault.

63
00:05:45,033 --> 00:05:48,635
He dies hours later with burns over almost his entire body.

64
00:05:48,635 --> 00:05:54,202
Twenty days before the flight that will make one of his friends the most famous man alive.

65
00:05:55,333 --> 00:05:58,533
And then, nothing. No announcement. No obituary.

66
00:05:58,533 --> 00:06:09,047
His file is sealed, his face is removed from group photographs, and his death is filed away as if it had never happened.

67
00:06:10,166 --> 00:06:16,721
The first man to die in the space race officially does not exist. Not for a year.

68
00:06:16,721 --> 00:06:19,805
Not for a decade. For twenty five years.

69
00:06:20,933 --> 00:06:23,412
Here is what that secret cost.

70
00:06:23,412 --> 00:06:30,851
Six years later, NASA seals three astronauts into a capsule full of pure oxygen for a routine test.

71
00:06:30,851 --> 00:06:37,050
Apollo 1. A spark. A fire. Three men dead before anyone could open the hatch.

72
00:06:38,199 --> 00:06:44,782
Some historians believe that if Bondarenko's death had been public, American engineers might have treated pure oxygen differently.

73
00:06:44,782 --> 00:06:50,634
The cover up did not just erase a man. It may have helped kill three more.

74
00:06:51,766 --> 00:06:55,737
Erasure number three. The man scratched out of the picture.

75
00:06:56,866 --> 00:06:58,370
Look at this photograph.

76
00:06:58,370 --> 00:07:02,504
The first cosmonaut group with Sergei Korolev, the chief designer himself.

77
00:07:02,504 --> 00:07:08,517
Gagarin sits at the center of history. Everyone in this frame is about to become immortal.

78
00:07:09,666 --> 00:07:14,854
Almost everyone. Because the official versions of photos like this were retouched.

79
00:07:14,854 --> 00:07:18,744
Faces painted over with bushes, with staircases, with sky.

80
00:07:18,744 --> 00:07:23,067
And one face was removed more often than any other.

81
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:25,925
Grigori Nelyubov. Fighter pilot.

82
00:07:25,925 --> 00:07:31,966
Brilliant, fast, and arrogant, with test scores among the best in the entire group.

83
00:07:31,966 --> 00:07:36,713
This is him, training for a flight he would never get.

84
00:07:37,833 --> 00:07:44,415
In April 1961, when Gagarin is strapped into Vostok 1, the state has prepared for every outcome.

85
00:07:44,415 --> 00:07:48,674
There are three pre-recorded announcements of the first man in space.

86
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:52,805
Gagarin flies. Titov is backup number one.

87
00:07:52,805 --> 00:07:59,673
And backup number two, the third name on that shortlist of all mankind, is Grigori Nelyubov.

88
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:06,063
He stands meters from the launch pad as his friend rides fire into the sky.

89
00:08:06,063 --> 00:08:11,327
Then he waits for his own turn. And waits. The flights go to other men.

90
00:08:12,466 --> 00:08:19,556
In March 1963, a drunken argument with a military patrol at a train station changes everything.

91
00:08:19,556 --> 00:08:24,430
The patrol demands an apology. Nelyubov, proud to the bone, refuses.

92
00:08:25,566 --> 00:08:32,889
For that, he is expelled from the cosmonaut corps and posted to a fighter regiment at the far eastern edge of the country.

93
00:08:32,889 --> 00:08:35,754
As far from Moscow as the Soviet Union goes.

94
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:41,288
But exile is not enough. The retouchers go to work.

95
00:08:41,288 --> 00:08:50,066
In the official record, in the published photographs, in the history of the space program, Nelyubov simply stops having existed.

96
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:58,156
Imagine what happens next. A man at a remote airbase tells his fellow pilots the truth.

97
00:08:58,156 --> 00:09:04,678
I was Gagarin's second backup. I nearly opened the space age. And nobody believes him.

98
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:11,299
Why would they? He is not in any photograph. He is not in any book.

99
00:09:11,299 --> 00:09:16,066
The state has made his greatest achievement look like a drunk man's fantasy.

100
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:25,162
On February 18th, 1966, Grigori Nelyubov is struck and killed by a train on a snowbound bridge in the far east.

101
00:09:25,162 --> 00:09:29,713
The military investigation notes he had spoken of taking his own life.

102
00:09:30,833 --> 00:09:35,796
He was thirty one. His name did not appear in print again for twenty years.

103
00:09:35,796 --> 00:09:41,752
The man third in line to open the space age was, for two decades, no one at all.

104
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:50,073
An accident erased. A death erased. A living man erased. The machine works perfectly.

105
00:09:50,073 --> 00:09:56,222
And then, in 1967, it meets a disaster too public to disappear.

106
00:09:57,366 --> 00:10:00,762
Erasure number four. The one they could not hide.

107
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:05,746
This is the payoff of everything you have watched so far.

108
00:10:05,746 --> 00:10:12,741
Because in April 1967, the Soviet cover up machine finally collides with a catastrophe in full view of the world.

109
00:10:13,866 --> 00:10:19,893
The fiftieth anniversary of the revolution is coming, and the leadership wants a triumph.

110
00:10:19,893 --> 00:10:24,628
Two ships docking in orbit. Cosmonauts crossing between them. A spectacle.

111
00:10:25,766 --> 00:10:32,402
The ship for the job is the brand new Soyuz. There is one problem. It is not ready.

112
00:10:32,402 --> 00:10:35,719
Its unmanned test flights have failed, one after another.

113
00:10:36,866 --> 00:10:40,307
The man assigned to fly it is Vladimir Komarov.

114
00:10:40,307 --> 00:10:45,658
Veteran cosmonaut, superb engineer, and one of the most respected men in the program.

115
00:10:45,658 --> 00:10:48,334
He knows exactly what the Soyuz is.

116
00:10:49,466 --> 00:10:54,073
By some accounts he told friends the flight would probably kill him.

117
00:10:54,073 --> 00:11:00,216
Asked why he did not refuse, he reportedly gave one reason. His backup would fly instead.

118
00:11:00,216 --> 00:11:02,135
His backup was Yuri Gagarin.

119
00:11:03,266 --> 00:11:04,915
Treat that story with care.

120
00:11:04,915 --> 00:11:09,201
It comes from disputed sources, and historians argue about it to this day.

121
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:12,827
What is not disputed is what the ship did to him.

122
00:11:13,966 --> 00:11:21,267
April 23rd. Soyuz 1 launches with Komarov alone aboard. Within hours the ship is crippled.

123
00:11:21,267 --> 00:11:28,568
One solar panel never opens. Power is failing. The ship will not hold its orientation.

124
00:11:29,700 --> 00:11:39,731
For a full day, Komarov flies a dying spacecraft by hand, spinning between Earth and sun, working every backup procedure in the book while the ground scrambles beneath him.

125
00:11:40,866 --> 00:11:48,255
The docking mission is abandoned. Now there is only one goal. Get him home. Reentry burn.

126
00:11:48,255 --> 00:11:53,797
Atmosphere. Parachute. Three steps between Komarov and the rest of his life.

127
00:11:54,933 --> 00:11:58,485
He survives the burn. He survives the plunge.

128
00:11:58,485 --> 00:12:07,367
And then the main parachute jams in its container, deformed, engineers later conclude, by faults nobody had time to find.

129
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:10,027
Komarov fires the reserve.

130
00:12:10,027 --> 00:12:19,576
It tangles instantly in the drogue of the failed main chute, and the capsule falls the last kilometers with nothing above it but knotted fabric.

131
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:27,475
Soyuz 1 hits the steppe near Orsk at roughly one hundred and forty kilometers per hour and bursts into flame.

132
00:12:27,475 --> 00:12:31,541
Vladimir Komarov becomes the first human being to die on a spaceflight.

133
00:12:32,666 --> 00:12:37,506
Now watch the pattern break. A hundred dead soldiers can be a plane crash.

134
00:12:37,506 --> 00:12:43,037
A burned trainee can be a sealed file. But the whole world watched Soyuz 1 launch.

135
00:12:44,166 --> 00:12:52,431
So for the first time in this story, the Soviet Union announces the death of a cosmonaut, hours after it happens.

136
00:12:52,431 --> 00:12:55,973
State funeral. Ashes in the Kremlin wall. National mourning.

137
00:12:57,100 --> 00:13:01,385
They could not erase the man. So they erased the reasons.

138
00:13:01,385 --> 00:13:08,008
The design flaws, the ignored warnings, the political deadline that put an unready ship on the pad.

139
00:13:08,008 --> 00:13:10,736
All of it stayed secret for decades.

140
00:13:11,866 --> 00:13:14,585
That is what erasure number four teaches.

141
00:13:14,585 --> 00:13:18,470
When hiding the body becomes impossible, you hide the cause.

142
00:13:18,470 --> 00:13:23,908
Remember that, because it happens again in four years, to three men at once.

143
00:13:25,033 --> 00:13:28,664
Erasure number five. Three men who never woke up.

144
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:30,608
June 1971.

145
00:13:30,608 --> 00:13:37,079
The Soviet Union has lost the Moon, so it plants its flag in a different frontier.

146
00:13:37,079 --> 00:13:40,719
Salyut 1. The first space station in human history.

147
00:13:41,866 --> 00:13:49,492
Aboard it, Georgi Dobrovolsky, Vladislav Volkov and Viktor Patsayev live for twenty three days, a world record.

148
00:13:49,492 --> 00:13:54,875
They give television tours. They are heroes broadcast into every Soviet home.

149
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,536
There is one detail that will matter.

150
00:13:58,536 --> 00:14:03,609
The Soyuz ferry is so cramped that the crew does not wear pressure suits.

151
00:14:03,609 --> 00:14:07,233
Three men, in shirtsleeves, in a machine full of valves.

152
00:14:08,366 --> 00:14:14,348
On June 29th they undock and head home. Retrofire is clean. Reentry is clean.

153
00:14:14,348 --> 00:14:20,330
The parachute opens. The capsule settles onto the Kazakh steppe like a textbook landing.

154
00:14:21,466 --> 00:14:29,512
The recovery crews run to the capsule, open the hatch, and find all three men in their couches, calm, composed, and dead.

155
00:14:30,633 --> 00:14:37,541
When the modules separated above the atmosphere, a pressure equalization valve was jolted open a hundred and sixty eight kilometers up.

156
00:14:37,541 --> 00:14:41,160
Their air hissed out into vacuum in less than a minute.

157
00:14:42,300 --> 00:14:50,479
Dobrovolsky, Volkov and Patsayev remain, to this day, the only human beings who have ever died in space itself.

158
00:14:50,479 --> 00:14:53,062
Above the line. In the black.

159
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:56,911
Once again the deaths cannot be hidden.

160
00:14:56,911 --> 00:15:01,172
The world had watched these men on television for three weeks.

161
00:15:01,172 --> 00:15:05,433
So once again, the state hides what it can. The cause.

162
00:15:06,566 --> 00:15:11,388
The valve, the vacuum, the shirtsleeves, the redesign that followed. Sealed.

163
00:15:11,388 --> 00:15:20,594
NASA, about to fly its own long duration crews, has to plead for details, and waits years for the full story.

164
00:15:21,733 --> 00:15:27,550
Since that day, no Soviet or Russian crew has ever flown reentry without a sealed pressure suit.

165
00:15:27,550 --> 00:15:31,999
The lesson was written in three lives, and then locked in a drawer.

166
00:15:33,133 --> 00:15:35,551
Five erasures. All of them, provably real.

167
00:15:35,551 --> 00:15:41,077
Which brings us, finally, back to the voice you heard at the beginning of this video.

168
00:15:41,077 --> 00:15:45,567
Erasure number six is the one that may never have happened at all.

169
00:15:46,700 --> 00:15:50,331
Erasure number six. The voices on the tapes.

170
00:15:51,466 --> 00:15:53,775
Turin, Italy. The late 1950s.

171
00:15:53,775 --> 00:16:03,012
Two brothers, Achille and Giovanni Judica-Cordiglia, build a listening station they call Torre Bert out of war surplus radio gear.

172
00:16:04,133 --> 00:16:10,458
They are amateurs, but they are not amateurs at listening. They pull down Sputnik's beep.

173
00:16:10,458 --> 00:16:12,145
They record American satellites.

174
00:16:12,145 --> 00:16:15,940
When Gagarin flies, they capture transmissions from that too.

175
00:16:17,066 --> 00:16:21,668
Which is exactly why what happens next is so hard to wave away.

176
00:16:21,668 --> 00:16:27,332
Because scattered among the real signals, the brothers begin logging things that match no announced mission.

177
00:16:28,466 --> 00:16:29,246
November 1960.

178
00:16:29,246 --> 00:16:34,704
They report what sounds like a distress code, tapped out from something moving overhead.

179
00:16:34,704 --> 00:16:38,993
A ship in trouble, where no ship is supposed to be.

180
00:16:40,133 --> 00:16:41,000
February 1961.

181
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:47,502
They record what they describe as the sound of a human being suffocating in orbit.

182
00:16:47,502 --> 00:16:51,403
A heartbeat, and failing breath, drifting across the sky.

183
00:16:51,403 --> 00:16:54,004
No mission is announced that day.

184
00:16:55,133 --> 00:16:59,756
And in May 1961, the recording that made Torre Bert famous.

185
00:16:59,756 --> 00:17:04,380
A woman's voice, in Russian, growing more desperate by the second.

186
00:17:05,499 --> 00:17:16,094
I am hot. I am hot. Am I going to crash? Yes. Yes. I am hot. I will re-enter.

187
00:17:16,094 --> 00:17:17,766
I will re-enter.

188
00:17:18,899 --> 00:17:27,561
If that recording is what it claims to be, a woman died in the sky two years before Valentina Tereshkova officially became the first woman in space.

189
00:17:27,561 --> 00:17:29,165
And history is a lie.

190
00:17:30,299 --> 00:17:31,646
So. Is it real?

191
00:17:31,646 --> 00:17:39,390
Here is the honest answer, and you deserve to hear it straight, because this video has shown you exactly why people believed it.

192
00:17:40,533 --> 00:17:45,717
The case against the tapes is strong. The voices break Soviet radio procedure.

193
00:17:45,717 --> 00:17:48,509
The orbital physics does not line up.

194
00:17:48,509 --> 00:17:52,497
Language experts hear mistakes no native Russian speaker would make.

195
00:17:53,633 --> 00:18:03,448
Decades of declassified Soviet records, opened after 1991, contain no dead cosmonauts before Komarov, no missing Vostoks, no erased orbital flights.

196
00:18:03,448 --> 00:18:06,720
Most historians consider the tapes a myth.

197
00:18:07,866 --> 00:18:12,250
But the myth refused to die, for one very good reason.

198
00:18:12,250 --> 00:18:21,815
Every time someone said the Soviet Union would never hide a dead spaceman, the Soviet Union was, at that exact moment, hiding dead spacemen.

199
00:18:22,933 --> 00:18:29,232
A hundred men at Baikonur. A plane crash that never happened. Bondarenko's sealed file.

200
00:18:29,232 --> 00:18:31,032
Nelyubov's scratched out face.

201
00:18:31,032 --> 00:18:36,882
The truth about Soyuz 1 and Soyuz 11, locked away for a generation.

202
00:18:37,999 --> 00:18:40,759
The lost cosmonauts are almost certainly not real.

203
00:18:40,759 --> 00:18:46,279
But the Soviet Union built the exact machine that would have hidden them if they were.

204
00:18:46,279 --> 00:18:49,728
That is not a conspiracy theory. That is this video.

205
00:18:50,866 --> 00:18:58,425
In the late 1980s, glasnost cracked the archives open, and the erased men came back one by one.

206
00:18:58,425 --> 00:19:01,785
Bondarenko got his obituary, twenty five years late.

207
00:19:02,933 --> 00:19:06,361
Nelyubov came back into the photographs and the history books.

208
00:19:06,361 --> 00:19:11,161
A crater on the far side of the Moon now quietly carries Komarov's name.

209
00:19:11,161 --> 00:19:16,647
Elsewhere on that same hidden hemisphere, three craters carry the names of the Apollo 1 crew.

210
00:19:17,766 --> 00:19:23,693
The men who fixed the R-16 on that launch pad have a memorial at Baikonur.

211
00:19:23,693 --> 00:19:29,619
Three years later, to the day, a second fire in a missile silo killed again.

212
00:19:29,619 --> 00:19:33,570
Since then, October 24th is Baikonur's black day. Nothing launches.

213
00:19:34,699 --> 00:19:38,107
And in Italy, the nine recordings still exist.

214
00:19:38,107 --> 00:19:45,775
Whatever they captured, a hoax, a mistake, or a voice, they remain the space race's strangest loose end.

215
00:19:46,899 --> 00:19:52,530
Here is the thought to leave with. Every space program is built on names we celebrate.

216
00:19:52,530 --> 00:19:58,863
This one was also built on names that had to be recovered, one by one, from deliberate silence.

217
00:19:59,999 --> 00:20:02,797
Gagarin's smile was real. The triumph was real.

218
00:20:02,797 --> 00:20:10,840
But behind the most famous photograph of the twentieth century stood men whose faces were sanded away so the picture would stay clean.

219
00:20:11,966 --> 00:20:15,947
History is not just what happened. It is what survived the retouching.

220
00:20:15,947 --> 00:20:21,919
And sometimes, the most important men in the picture are the ones who are no longer in it.

221
00:20:23,066 --> 00:20:27,190
If this story gripped you, the next one is already waiting.

222
00:20:27,190 --> 00:20:32,813
Every way space has actually killed a human, the full story, is on screen now.

223
00:20:32,813 --> 00:20:36,937
And subscribe to mindwired, so the next erased story finds you.

